WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:05.521
"In fourteen hundred and ninety-two, Columbus sailed the ocean blue."

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:13.568
With this simple rhyme, many students memorize the date for the discovery of the Americas by Christopher Columbus.

3
00:00:13.569 --> 00:00:24.555
The story of this brave man is often told today to remind people of the courage of the early European explorers and of their voyages to the New World.

4
00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:34.423
Although there are few accounts of his early life, it is believed that Christopher Columbus was born in Genoa, Italy, in 1451.

5
00:00:34.424 --> 00:00:38.872
In his teenage years, he became an experienced sailor.

6
00:00:38.873 --> 00:00:46.159
At 25 he was hired to join an armed convoy to northern Europe to deliver valuable cargo.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.754
This would prove to be an important event in Columbus’ life.

8
00:00:50.755 --> 00:00:56.866
As the convoy was sailing along the coast, it was intercepted by French privateers.

9
00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:02.808
Luckily Columbus survived by swimming almost ten kilometers to shore.

10
00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:09.292
This experience, however, was just one example of the many dangers that merchants faced. Unfortunately

11
00:01:09.293 --> 00:01:20.781
navigation technology was very simple at the time, and many sailors were reluctant to travel too far from land, where they would lose sight of the shore. The

12
00:01:20.782 --> 00:01:24.842
land route to the East was also an arduous one. Those

13
00:01:24.843 --> 00:01:32.484
who bought spices and silk from Asia and sold them in Europe were desperate to find an alternative trade route. This

14
00:01:32.485 --> 00:01:36.878
situation gave Columbus an outstanding idea. What

15
00:01:36.879 --> 00:01:43.201
if it was possible to sail directly across the Atlantic Ocean from Europe to Asia? By

16
00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:48.199
the fifteenth century, there was ample evidence that the Earth was round. All

17
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:56.208
he needed were ships to command, men to serve as crewmembers, and enough food and supplies for the length of the voyage. So

18
00:01:56.209 --> 00:02:04.238
the enterprising Italian set out to find a benefactor who would pay for an expedition to test his prediction. The

19
00:02:04.239 --> 00:02:13.184
charismatic Columbus was easily able to gain audiences with royal families, but most declined to fund his proposal. Everyone

20
00:02:13.185 --> 00:02:17.785
thought it was unsafe to travel such a long distance. No

21
00:02:17.786 --> 00:02:22.671
ship, they said, could carry enough food to sustain its crew. But

22
00:02:22.672 --> 00:02:25.345
Columbus was determined. Finally,

23
00:02:25.346 --> 00:02:34.952
after several years, Columbus convinced the rulers of Spain, King Ferdinand and Queen Isabella, to support his expedition. They

24
00:02:34.953 --> 00:02:45.484
wanted access to valuable trade with China, so even though there were still some concerns about Columbus’ plan, Spain was willing to take the risk. They

25
00:02:45.485 --> 00:02:53.944
offered him three vessels, the Niña , the Pinta , and the Santa Maria , and paid for a crew

26
00:02:53.945 --> 00:02:58.748
and supplies. On August 3, 1492, Columbus

27
00:02:58.749 --> 00:03:11.130
set sail. Both the Niña and the Pinta were light, fast, and maneuverable, whereas the Santa Maria , which carried all of their cargo, was longer, wider, and slower than

28
00:03:11.131 --> 00:03:14.849
the other two. The expedition was expected to

29
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:24.517
take several weeks. Columbus was quite confident. He had learned everything about sailing while he was a young boy, and read everything he could

30
00:03:24.518 --> 00:03:32.733
about navigation. He knew, for example, that at certain latitudes, the wind tends to blow in a constant direction, either east

31
00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:39.279
or west. Columbus could rely on these prevailing winds to carry him back and forth between Europe

32
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:45.833
and Asia. His crewmembers, on the other hand, were inexperienced and many had never

33
00:03:45.834 --> 00:03:53.603
sailed before. Some of them were convicts who had agreed to participate in the voyage only because they had been offered their freedom

34
00:03:53.604 --> 00:04:02.969
in exchange. They had no knowledge of prevailing winds and thought that, since the wind was constantly blowing them west, they would never be able to

35
00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.982
return home. This made them

36
00:04:05.983 --> 00:04:13.410
very uneasy. They stopped at the Canary Islands, where the crew picked up provisions such as fresh water, wine,

37
00:04:13.411 --> 00:04:23.684
and wood. Food on board the ships mostly consisted of items that could be preserved: cheese, chickpeas, almonds, salted fish, and beef

38
00:04:23.685 --> 00:04:26.580
and pork. Then they continued

39
00:04:26.581 --> 00:04:34.026
sailing westward. After 34 days on the open water, they had still not sighted land, and the crew was

40
00:04:34.027 --> 00:04:40.137
quite anxious. They were ready to mutiny, but Columbus convinced them to continue west for three

41
00:04:40.138 --> 00:04:43.514
more days. The very next day, land

42
00:04:43.515 --> 00:04:47.463
was sighted. On October 12 they dropped anchor and

43
00:04:47.464 --> 00:04:55.160
went ashore. Relieved that he had avoided mutiny, Columbus named it San Salvador Island, meaning

44
00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:58.546
"holy savior." Today this island is part of

45
00:04:58.547 --> 00:05:04.106
the Bahamas. After some exploration they returned to their ships and continued on

46
00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:12.536
their journey. They visited Cuba and the island of Hispaniola, which today contains the countries Haiti and the

47
00:05:12.537 --> 00:05:18.153
Dominican Republic. At each place Columbus and his crew encountered many

48
00:05:18.154 --> 00:05:24.199
native tribes. He referred to them as "Indians" in the mistaken belief that his ships were off the coast of

49
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:29.029
India. To this day Native Americans are still sometimes called

50
00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:33.642
Indians. Columbus was preparing to return home when a catastrophe

51
00:05:33.643 --> 00:05:38.133
occurred. The Santa Maria hit a coral reef and could not

52
00:05:38.134 --> 00:05:50.613
continue. There was not enough room on the Niña and the Pinta to carry the extra crew, so Columbus left 39 of his men behind with instructions to establish a fort on

53
00:05:50.614 --> 00:05:57.850
Hispaniola. Then he set sail for Europe, arriving in Spain on March 15,

54
00:05:57.851 --> 00:06:02.103
1493. The Spanish deemed Columbus’ expedition a

55
00:06:02.104 --> 00:06:10.157
triumph. He had traded with the Native Americans for tobacco, pineapples, and gold that he brought back for the king and

56
00:06:10.158 --> 00:06:12.114
queen. They were very

57
00:06:12.115 --> 00:06:16.547
pleased. Word of his discovery spread rapidly throughout the rest of

58
00:06:16.548 --> 00:06:25.365
Europe. King Ferdinand immediately offered the explorer the opportunity to return to the islands, which Columbus believed were part of

59
00:06:25.366 --> 00:06:32.810
Asia. In an accord between Columbus and the king, the explorer was given the difficult task of colonizing the

60
00:06:32.811 --> 00:06:36.806
region. Columbus made three more voyages to the New

61
00:06:36.807 --> 00:06:44.264
World. In the first, he commanded 17 ships that carried 1,200 settlers for the new

62
00:06:44.265 --> 00:06:52.346
colonies. He was away for almost a year and explored many more islands in the Caribbean, including Jamaica and Puerto

63
00:06:52.347 --> 00:06:58.177
Rico. He also discovered the mainland of South America, which he believed to be

64
00:06:58.178 --> 00:07:03.571
China. Columbus’ next voyage, in 1498, was much less

65
00:07:03.572 --> 00:07:08.282
successful. The colonies that he had started on his previous voyage were in

66
00:07:08.283 --> 00:07:14.678
disarray. Some had been destroyed in disputes with natives; others had fallen apart in hunger and

67
00:07:14.679 --> 00:07:24.269
despair. In fact, the colonies were such a failure that the Spanish monarchy sent a ship to arrest Columbus; he was brought back to Spain in

68
00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:30.705
chains. Although the charges were later dropped, Columbus was never again allowed to govern a Spanish

69
00:07:30.706 --> 00:07:35.877
colony. In his fourth voyage, Columbus returned to the New World one final time.

70
00:07:35.878 --> 00:07:43.519
Unfortunately his ships were marooned in Jamaica because of a storm, and it was a year before help arrived.

71
00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:49.987
Upon his return to Europe, Columbus used the gold he had amassed over the years to retire.

72
00:07:49.988 --> 00:07:54.959
Today the legacy of Columbus’ actions is quite controversial.

73
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.647
For many Americans, Columbus’ achievements make him a hero.

74
00:07:59.648 --> 00:08:05.732
He was the first to bravely travel across the Atlantic and open the Americas to the West.

75
00:08:05.733 --> 00:08:12.025
For others, especially Native Americans, Columbus is viewed as little more than a criminal.

76
00:08:12.026 --> 00:08:20.281
He enslaved many of them and stole their natural resources by chopping down their trees and stealing their gold, for example.

77
00:08:20.282 --> 00:08:26.159
Additionally his arrival brought deadly new diseases from Europe that killed millions.

78
00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:37.988
While the debate will no doubt continue, perhaps it is best to remember Columbus for the qualities that made him extraordinary: his courage, his skill, and his ambition.

